-HTTPX will use universal line endings, normalising all cases to `\n`.
+By default, `iter_lines()` and `iter_lines(keepends=False)` remove line endings,
+just like `str.splitlines()`. Pass `keepends=True` to retain the original endings,
+including `\r\n`, `\r`, `\n`, and all other separators recognized by
+`str.splitlines(keepends=True)`.
+
+```pycon
+>>> r = httpx.Response(200, text="first\r\nsecond\rlast")
+>>> list(r.iter_lines())
+['first', 'second', 'last']
+>>> list(r.iter_lines(keepends=True))
+['first\r\n', 'second\r', 'last']
+```
+
+The same option is available on `aiter_lines()`. Both methods support cached and
+streaming responses, yielding lines incrementally. A trailing `\r` is held until
+more text arrives or the stream ends, so a split `\r\n` stays together.
+Empty responses yield no lines, and a final line without a line ending is returned
+unchanged.
 
 In some cases you might want to access the raw bytes on the response without applying any HTTP content decoding. In this case any content encoding that the web server has applied such as `gzip`, `deflate`, `brotli`, or `zstd` will
 not be automatically decoded.
